CS 499 Milestone Two Narrative
The artifact selected for my ePortfolio is the Android Weight Tracking application originally developed in CS-360: Mobile Architecture and Programming. The application was created during my coursework to demonstrate my understanding of mobile application development, user interface design, database integration, and application functionality. The application allows users to create an account, securely log in, record weight entries, establish a weight goal, and view their progress through a dashboard interface.
For my capstone project, I renamed the application Elevate to better represent its purpose as a personal goal-tracking application. This artifact was selected because it represents several important areas of computer science that I developed throughout my program, including software engineering practices, mobile application development, database management, and user-centered design.
I selected this artifact because it provided the best opportunity to demonstrate my growth as a software developer. The application already contained important functionality, including user authentication, SQLite database storage, RecyclerView implementation, and multiple activity screens. However, reviewing the original code helped me identify areas where the software architecture could be improved.
The primary enhancement completed for this milestone focused on improving the software design and engineering of the application. Specifically, I refactored the DashboardActivity class to improve organization, readability, and maintainability. The original implementation successfully performed the required functions, but several responsibilities were handled within the same activity. The enhanced version separates tasks into dedicated methods responsible for initialization, user data retrieval, navigation, database loading, and user feedback.
Additional improvements included strengthening error handling, improving validation of user information, reducing repeated code, and creating reusable methods for common actions such as displaying messages and managing navigation. These changes make the application easier for future developers to understand, modify, and expand.
The enhanced DashboardActivity demonstrates software development skills by applying principles such as modular design, separation of responsibilities, code readability, and maintainable programming practices. Rather than only focusing on adding new features, the enhancement improved the foundation of the application so that future functionality can be added more efficiently.
The planned enhancement from Module One was designed to align with Course Outcomes 2, 3, and 4. After completing this enhancement, I believe these outcomes were successfully demonstrated.
Course Outcome 2 was demonstrated through improving the organization and documentation of the application code. By restructuring methods and adding clearer comments, the application became easier for other developers and stakeholders to understand.
Course Outcome 3 was demonstrated by evaluating the existing application structure and making design decisions that improved the way the application manages user interactions and application flow. The enhancement required analyzing the existing implementation and determining where changes would provide the greatest improvement.
Course Outcome 4 was demonstrated through applying software engineering practices to improve the quality of an existing application. Refactoring the DashboardActivity, reducing duplicated logic, and improving maintainability reflect industry-standard development practices.
At this stage, I believe the enhancement successfully addresses the software engineering category. My future enhancements in the algorithms and database categories will expand on this foundation by improving data organization, calculations, and database functionality.
The process of enhancing this application helped me better understand that software development involves more than simply creating functionality that works. A well-designed application must also be maintainable, understandable, and adaptable to future changes. Through this enhancement, I learned the importance of organizing code in a way that allows developers to easily identify responsibilities and make improvements without introducing unnecessary complexity.
One challenge I encountered was determining which improvements would provide meaningful value while staying aligned with the software engineering category. Since the application was already functional, the goal was not to completely rewrite the application but to improve the existing design. This required reviewing the original implementation, identifying areas of improvement, and making changes that strengthened the application's overall structure.
Another challenge was balancing improvements to the code with maintaining the original functionality. Refactoring existing code requires careful testing to ensure that changes improve the application without creating new issues. Through this process, I gained more experience analyzing existing software, identifying design weaknesses, and applying professional development practices.
